SUMMARY:Hidden Mythologies: Erzya & The Mari Republic
DESCRIPTION:Event Details:    Now in its second year\, the Hidden Mytholo
 gies lecture series explores the rich and often overlooked mythological t
 raditions of the peoples of the Volga-Ural region and beyond. Through sto
 ries of gods\, spirits\, legendary heroes\, sacred landscapes\, and tradi
 tional beliefs\, the series examines the ways mythology reflects and pres
 erves the cultures and worldviews of diverse communities across Eurasia. 
      This program explores the rich mythological traditions of the Erzya 
 people\, including Ineshkipaz\, Cham-Pas\, Shkai\, and Ange-Patyai\, and 
 invites guests to&#160\;discover the fall rituals and wedding customs wit
 h guest speaker\, Erzya Cultural Practitioner and blogger Vlasta Pilot. T
 he program will conclude with a performance of Erzyan folk song Kov purna
 t-sernyat by a music group from Saransk\, Toorama.      Each program begi
 ns with a presentation by Dr. Gulshat Safiullina exploring an aspect of t
 he featured culture's mythological traditions\, followed by a musical per
 formance curated by musical director Daryana Antipova to bring another di
 mension to the stories\, beliefs\, and cultural heritage explored in the 
 lecture.      This event is held in collaboration with the Slavic and Eas
 t European Languages Department\, the Jacobs School of Music\, and the CH
 AKCHAK: Languages and Cultures Foundation.  Parking Information:Limitedco
 mplimentary parkingis available in front of theMuseum entranceoff Indiana
  Avenue. Street parking is free on weekends and weekdays after 5:00 PM in
  the neighborhoods surrounding the Museum. Paid parking options include t
 heUniversity Collections at McCallaparking lot off Indiana Avenue\, theIn
 diana Memorial Unionvisitor lot\, and nearby metered street parking.Conve
 nient metered street parking is available in the downtown/Sample Gates ar
 ea along Kirkwood Avenue\, East 6th Street\, East 4th Street\, North Linc
 oln Street\, North Dunn Street\, and South Grant Street.Questions?  For a
 ccessibility assistance or questions about this event\, please reach out 
 to Camden Hill at camrhill@iu.edu (mailto:camrhill@iu.edu) or (812) 855-1
 696.Note:  By attending this event\, you agree to be in photography that 
 may be used by the University for promotional purposes. If you would not 
 like to be in any pictures\, please speak to a Museum staff member and th
 ey will inform assigned photographers of your request. If you see that yo
 ur image has been used in error\, please reach out to iumaa@iu.edu (mailt
 o:iumaa@iu.edu) to request that it be taken down.
